      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To assess the outcome of patients who underwent lumbar discectomy for sciatica as determined by return to work, residual functional disability and self reported pain measures."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Ninety seven patients who had undergone L4-5 or L5-S1 discectomies for sciatica were reviewed 20 months after surgery. Variables assessed were: gender; length of preoperative time off work and compensation claims for treatment. Outcome was determined by current earning capacity, residual pain level and the Oswestry disability questionnaire."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The length of preoperative sick leave had a significant effect on all outcome measures of function, pain, ability to return to full time work and current compensation status. Those patients who had more than 6 months sick leave prior to operation were less likely to have a favourable outcome."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "While chronicity of symptoms does not negate the indications for discectomy, the results suggest that the duration of preoperative leave needs to be considered prior to operation."
        }
      ]},
